TRANSPILATION:
  ☐ Figure out TODOs
  ☐ BUG/GAP (found 2026-08-03 while writing equivalence tests): the standalone
    `transpile_source` path rejects two constructs the macro-sim accepts, with
    "cannot infer bit width; add an explicit type annotation":
      - `[Logic::Zero; N]` array locals (raw Logic arrays; `Bits::zero()` +
        indexed writes works instead)
      - tuple-returning plain-Rust helper fns called from hardware bodies
        (e.g. `let (s, c) = full_adder(...)`; inlining the expression works)
    Concretely, examples/combinational/ripple_carry_adder.rs and
    examples/basejump/bsg_gray_to_binary.rs (as written) do NOT round-trip through
    `transpile_source` / the copper-transpile CLI — they are only ever checked via
    the macro-sim + hand-written SV in their own main(). The new equivalence tests
    (tests/ripple_carry_adder_equivalence.rs, tests/gray_to_binary_equivalence.rs)
    use Bits-indexing rewrites of the same designs to get real sim≡SV coverage;
    the underlying transpiler inference gap is NOT fixed, just worked around.
  ✔ FIXED (2026-08-03): Moore output decoded from a state `case` was lowered into
    `always_ff` (REGISTERED, `out <= …`) instead of `always_comb`, lagging the
    simulator one cycle (verilator FAIL / trace PASS).
      TRUE ROOT CAUSE (my first "single-register" hypothesis was wrong): the trigger
      is a NON-EXHAUSTIVE state `case` — a register whose encoding leaves unreachable
      values (6 states in a 3-bit reg; a `u8` pc with 2 states). ports_driven_all_paths
      refused to count ANY output as fully driven on a case with no default that isn't
      exhaustive, so an output written on every explicit arm still looked "conditional"
      and got moved to always_ff. traffic_light is immune only because 4 states = 2^2
      is exactly exhaustive. (Nothing to do with register count.)
      FIX: copper-codegen/src/vlir_lower.rs `hoist_moore_output_defaults` — for an
      output that is currently conditional AND driven on every explicit arm, hoist a
      top-of-`always_comb` default (`out = <first-arm value>;`) and give the case an
      empty `default` (Verilator CASEINCOMPLETE is an error). Narrow by design:
      exhaustive cases (traffic_light) are byte-identical; a genuine enabled-register
      hold driven in only some arms (bsg_dff_en) is untouched and stays `always_ff`
      (both verified). seq6 → full equivalence (tests/fsm_six_state_equivalence.rs).
      RESIDUAL (separate, still open): see the Mealy-output item directly below.
  ☐ BUG / OPEN (flagged 2026-08-03, surfaced while fixing the Moore-output bug
    above): a MEALY FSM output — one that depends on a live input inside a state,
    not only on the state register — does not reconcile between the simulator and
    the transpiled SV. After the Moore fix, if_tick lowers to a correct-looking
    combinational `always_comb` (`pc=0: out_o = sel ? 1 : 0`), yet Verilator still
    disagrees with the sim at specific cycles (trace PASS / verilator FAIL).
      Repro: tests/fixtures/control_extraction_dut.rs (`if_tick`) — branch-nested
      tick where the `then` path writes out under a live `sel` read. The two SIM
      forms agree (if_tick ≡ if_tick_explicit), so this is a sim-vs-SV *timing*
      reconciliation for Mealy outputs, NOT the (now-fixed) registered-Moore bug.
      This is the same family as the long-standing output-timing reconciliation the
      control_extraction structural test deliberately sidesteps (it compares SV
      shape, not behavior). Needs a decision on the canonical Mealy-output edge
      timing before it can be fixed — a CODEGEN and/or SIM change (get sign-off).
      Meanwhile tests/nested_tick_equivalence.rs runs sim_only.

